    <title>Language Models Can Rewrite Their Own Weights</title>
    <link href="https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/?item=2026-11-21-10-language-models-can-rewrite-their"/>
    <link rel="related" href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21885"/>
    <id>https://latifkedi.github.io/ahbar/item/2026-11-21-10-language-models-can-rewrite-their</id>
    <updated>2026-11-20T16:29:06Z</updated>
    <summary type="html">&lt;p&gt;Language models are trained once, frozen and deployed: improving one again takes another training run, and a prompt, a scaffold or extra computation at inference is gone once the task ends. A frontier model has been shown to edit the weights of a much smaller one. We ask whether a model can rewrite its own weights to make itself better, and find that it can, by its own reasoning, with no gradients and no training; we call this self-rewriting.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Source:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.21885&quot;&gt;Hacker News&lt;/a&gt; · &lt;strong&gt;Tags:&lt;/strong&gt; #software-architecture #open-source&lt;/p&gt;</summary>
  </entry>
